# .gitignore — hidden by convention; Git itself looks for this exact name.
#
# The leading dot is mandated by Git: renaming this file would break
# discovery. That's the strongest case for a dotfile — the tool owns the
# name and you don't have a choice. The rule of thumb still applies:
# contributors rarely *edit* this file by hand; they edit it once when a
# new ignore pattern is needed, then forget it exists.
#
# Realistic patterns below illustrate what typically lives here.

# --- Environment & secrets ---------------------------------------------
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# --- Build outputs (regeneratable) -------------------------------------
dist/
build/
out/
target/
_site/
public/

# --- Dependency caches -------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# --- Editor / IDE state ------------------------------------------------
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~

# --- OS noise ----------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
